20:- A `beforeSession` that throws is a failed turn like any other: the throw reaches the same `catch` and `finally` as a provider failure, and the caller still sees the rejection unchanged ([validated by: propagates a beforeSession throw to the caller as a rejection](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.beforeSession.acceptance.test.ts#L55)).
21:- `onError` fires for that throw with the error `beforeSession` threw, in the same argument shape as any other orchestrator failure ([validated by: hands onError the error a beforeSession threw](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.beforeSession.acceptance.test.ts#L63)).
22:- `afterSession` fires for that throw too, and a hook can assume the turn ended before any of it happened: no user input was rewritten, no tool ran and the provider was never called. The recorded answer on cleanup is that `afterSession` fires either way and cannot tell a session whose `beforeSession` threw from one that ran to completion — it receives the session and nothing else — so a hook that releases in `afterSession` what `beforeSession` allocated must tolerate releasing when the allocation never happened ([validated by: runs afterSession when beforeSession throws](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.beforeSession.acceptance.test.ts#L72)).
